WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.905
Your pod restarts, and every file it wrote is gone.

2
00:00:03.925 --> 00:00:06.617
A container starts with a clean disk each time.

3
00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:08.465
That is by design.

4
00:00:08.485 --> 00:00:13.290
This port has three steps: volumes, claims, and StatefulSets.

5
00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.925
First: storage that outlives a crash.

6
00:00:15.945 --> 00:00:19.037
A volume belongs to the pod, not to the container.

7
00:00:19.057 --> 00:00:21.135
A crash does not clear it.

8
00:00:21.155 --> 00:00:23.810
But the simplest volume is deleted with the pod.

9
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.110
It is scratch space.

10
00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.795
Second: storage that outlives the pod.

11
00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:30.345
Your app asks for a size.

12
00:00:30.365 --> 00:00:32.105
It does not name a disk.

13
00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:34.005
A storage class makes the disk.

14
00:00:34.025 --> 00:00:36.855
The claim binds to it and stays.

15
00:00:36.875 --> 00:00:39.340
Third: a database needs a name.

16
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.250
A StatefulSet gives each pod a fixed name, and its own disk.

17
00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:44.525
Pods start in order.

18
00:00:44.545 --> 00:00:47.600
And when you delete the set, the disks stay.

19
00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.535
Quick recap of storage.

20
00:00:49.555 --> 00:00:50.723
Containers forget.

21
00:00:50.743 --> 00:00:51.973
Claims remember.

22
00:00:51.993 --> 00:00:54.595
StatefulSets give a name and a disk.

23
00:00:54.615 --> 00:00:57.715
Now start the first lesson below.
